{"author":{"id":"6d85cf2fcebaeefd8c5c528f2d5f5f98f26ce3275422d0dc5f7c0fd596f9f59f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792163143,"nonce":"OWNTR5VU1c3QuxAgFxXOkv5CpD0=","title":"A backdrop draws its own shadow behind it","message":"A box with a glass or blur background (and so backdrop-filter) pushes its outer shadows before its backdrop record, so the backdrop it captures includes its own shadow. Liquid glass's rim bends what lies just outside the box into it, so a box-shadow under a lens shows as a dark band inside the rim; frosted glass and backdrop blur spread it inside too. In a browser the backdrop is what was painted before the element, which its own shadow is not; upstream Blinc draws a glass element's shadow in the glass shader, outside the shape, so its backdrop never holds it.\n\nReproducer: a 160px round lens (background: glass) with box-shadow: 0 18px 40px rgba(0,0,0,0.35) over a solid backdrop, rendered with and without the shadow: 1,695 pixels inside the lens differ (liquid), 6,063 (frosted), up to 13 levels. Over stripes, 5,679.\n\nFix: for a box with a backdrop, push its outer shadows after the backdrop record. An outer shadow is cut out inside its box, so it looks the same outside it, and the backdrop no longer holds it.","files":null}]}